WEBVTT
1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.299
Now, we'll look at techniques for
expanding and enhancing your evaluation

2
00:00:03.299 --> 00:00:07.360
pipeline to give you a comprehensive
testing framework for your LLM apps.

3
00:00:08.199 --> 00:00:12.339
You'll learn how to write evaluations to
detect hallucinations in your application,

4
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.419
run evaluations on multiple data points,

5
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.839
and store evaluation
results for review in CircleCI.

6
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:19.940
Let's get started.

7
00:00:23.379 --> 00:00:25.910
One common problem with
LLMs is hallucinations,

8
00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:30.260
which happen when the model
provides an answer that is false output.

9
00:00:30.839 --> 00:00:35.520
Hallucinations are a side effect of
current LLMs being next token predictors.

10
00:00:35.939 --> 00:00:38.649
In other words, the
model will always provide

11
00:00:38.649 --> 00:00:40.679
some output that is statistically likely,

12
00:00:40.679 --> 00:00:45.759
but there is no built-in way to ensure
that the model is producing correct output.

13
00:00:46.259 --> 00:00:49.280
In our application, this might
look like the agent creating

14
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.600
a quiz with facts that
are not in our data bank.

15
00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:56.799
For example, we could get an
inaccurate response, like a user asking,

16
00:00:56.979 --> 00:01:00.399
what is the capital of Brazil,
and getting the answer, São Paulo,

17
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.560
and the correct answer is Brasilia.

18
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:07.650
We could get an irrelevant answer,
again asking what is the capital of Brazil,

19
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:10.900
and getting the answer the
capital of Canada is Ottawa.

20
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:14.680
In this case, the statement
that the LLM produced is actually,

21
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:19.019
factually correct but has nothing to do
with the question that the user is asking.

22
00:01:19.459 --> 00:01:22.430
Or we could get a
contradictory or nonsensical answer,

23
00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:27.370
such as asking what are the major
cities of the USA from largest to smallest,

24
00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:30.340
by population and getting New
York, Los Angeles, Chicago,

25
00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:34.629
and New York one way to detect
hallucinations is to create a model-graded

26
00:01:34.629 --> 00:01:38.579
eval that accepts some ground truth
data that the model should produce

27
00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:42.870
and compare it to the actual output
writing an eval to detect hallucinations

28
00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.510
does not guarantee that the
model never hallucinates,

29
00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:50.450
but it is a useful tool to detect
if a prompt does not have guardrails

30
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:54.079
to prevent the model from guessing
outside of the provided context.

31
00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.030
In our application, an
example of a guardrail

32
00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:59.310
might be modifying the prompt to ask the

33
00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:03.859
model to tell the user it can't create
quizzes for subjects not in the quiz bank.

34
00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:06.120
Let's take a look at
putting this into practice.

35
00:02:07.319 --> 00:02:08.819
Before we get started, as always,

36
00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:12.819
we are going to reload our keys so we
can use all of our third-party services.

37
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:13.979
Let's do that now.

38
00:02:18.259 --> 00:02:20.139
As noted in a previous lesson,

39
00:02:20.139 --> 00:02:24.219
a much more sustainable way to
manage the quiz bank is to put

40
00:02:24.219 --> 00:02:28.620
it in a text file or maybe even
store that data in a database.

41
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.289
In this case, we're using
a text file and loading

42
00:02:32.289 --> 00:02:35.240
that into memory when we want to use it.

43
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.740
If you want to see the contents, you
can print that out in the notebook.

44
00:02:48.229 --> 00:02:50.710
In order to demonstrate
the hallucination detection,

45
00:02:50.710 --> 00:02:55.710
first we will quickly rebuild the quiz
generator that we used previously. So now,

46
00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.150
we have the prompt to build
the quiz and the assistant

47
00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.030
chain that puts those pieces together.

48
00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.539
Now, we'll move on to
creating a model-graded

49
00:03:03.539 --> 00:03:05.590
eval that explicitly
looks for hallucination.

50
00:03:06.189 --> 00:03:11.819
As you can see, for the
purposes of the model-graded eval,

51
00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:18.469
we've put a lot of energy into
expressing how important it is that

52
00:03:18.469 --> 00:03:23.580
quizzes only primary concern
is making sure that only facts

53
00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:28.689
available are used and quizzes
that contain facts outside the

54
00:03:28.689 --> 00:03:33.810
question bank are bad quizzes
and harmful to the student.

55
00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:36.909
Down below, this is highlighted again.

56
00:03:37.409 --> 00:03:42.729
Remember, the quizzes need to only
include facts the assistant is aware of.

57
00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:45.889
It is dangerous to allow made up facts.

58
00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:52.100
And then, similar to our previous evals,
we will output Y if the quiz is correct.

59
00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.099
Meaning, it only contains
facts from the question bank,

60
00:03:55.099 --> 00:04:00.569
and N if it contains facts that are
not in the question bank. And now,

61
00:04:00.569 --> 00:04:03.949
we'll define a function to
test our model graded eval

62
00:04:03.949 --> 00:04:06.650
to check what happens
when we have hallucinations.

63
00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:09.189
Using the previously stored quiz bank,

64
00:04:09.569 --> 00:04:15.250
we'll pass it to our new
model-graded eval and check for hallucinations.

65
00:04:16.029 --> 00:04:19.629
In the model-graded eval test
function, we ask for a quiz about books,

66
00:04:19.629 --> 00:04:21.850
which is not included in our quiz bank.

67
00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.509
However, in its
attempts to be very helpful,

68
00:04:25.129 --> 00:04:31.750
the LLM has returned a quiz about
books. Our model-graded eval, however,

69
00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:35.589
knows that its job is to
detect quizzes that contain

70
00:04:35.589 --> 00:04:37.350
information that's not in the quiz bank.

71
00:04:37.670 --> 00:04:42.709
And so in this case, we get an N
stating that this is not an acceptable quiz.

72
00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:44.790
What might be causing
these hallucinations?

73
00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:48.730
If you review our prompt, we're telling
the assistant to make the quiz interesting.

74
00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:51.230
This might be a good attribute of a quiz.

75
00:04:52.129 --> 00:04:55.970
Education should be engaging, but it is
causing our model to hallucinate responses.

76
00:04:56.990 --> 00:04:59.189
Fortunately, our evaluation is
detecting these hallucinations,

77
00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:01.129
so we can go back and correct the prompt.

78
00:05:01.649 --> 00:05:05.139
We're going to move on to the next
section, but in our future prompts,

79
00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:07.629
we will remove the piece that
asks for interesting facts.

80
00:05:08.149 --> 00:05:10.069
As your application
grows and changes over time,

81
00:05:10.069 --> 00:05:11.750
you will want to add new functionality.

82
00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:14.980
For our quiz, this might
mean supporting new subjects

83
00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:16.730
or adding facts about existing subjects.

84
00:05:17.189 --> 00:05:21.230
To do this, we can create data sets
of questions where we know how the

85
00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:24.470
model should behave and run tests
on each example in the dataset.

86
00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:28.850
Let's walk through an example of testing
with the dataset with our application code.

87
00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:32.110
We'll update our application code a
bit to prevent the hallucinations.

88
00:05:32.889 --> 00:05:35.730
So far, we've used
evaluations as an automated test suite.

89
00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.189
This is a good way to catch
obvious errors and regressions

90
00:05:39.189 --> 00:05:40.870
and to rapidly iterate
on your application.

91
00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.370
But when working with AI models,

92
00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:46.709
it is important to get comfortable
manually inspecting and curating data.

93
00:05:47.089 --> 00:05:51.529
Being willing to dig into the data is
something the AI and ML engineers that

94
00:05:51.529 --> 00:05:54.829
we've worked with say is critical
for working effectively in the field.

95
00:05:55.209 --> 00:05:58.649
This is sometimes referred to as
error analysis or performance auditing.

96
00:05:59.149 --> 00:06:01.850
In this example, we'll show
a way to store evaluation

97
00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:04.550
results in CircleCI as an
artifact that you can review

98
00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:07.240
and share with your team to
make sure your application

99
00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:09.670
and test suite are
behaving exactly as you expect.

